= latch-port-must-be-numeric
f libexec/mux-latch
t mux-latch
# Widening the pattern so a word passes as a port turns the old spelling
# `box:work` into a host `box` on a port `work`: dialled, plausible and wrong,
# instead of refused with the migration. A no-op mutation was tried first here
# (adding a character to the negated class that the fixture never contains),
# which SURVIVED and proved nothing: the replacement has to change the path
# the fixture actually takes.
-   *[!0-9]*)
+   *[!0-9a-z]*)
m pre-0.84 target form must exit 2

= latch-ipv6-is-not-split
f libexec/mux-latch
t mux-latch
# Requiring a THIRD colon sends `fe80::1` down the single-colon arm, where the
# tail is digits, so it splits into host `fe80:` and port `1`, a host that
# does not exist, dialled while looking like it worked.
- *:*:*)
+ *:*:*:*)
m IPv6 literal must be left whole

= undo-pane-unwrapped-display-form
f libexec/mux-undo-pane
t mux-undo-pane
# tmux 3.4 escapes `pane_start_command` WITHOUT wrapping it in quotes, 3.6 wraps
# and escapes. Treating the wrapper as the signal returned 3.4's form verbatim,
# so a restored pane exec'd a program literally named `${SHELL:-/bin/sh}`:
# created, silent, wrong, on every Ubuntu LTS box. The assertion is a unit on
# BOTH measured literals and sits AHEAD of the tmux skip, so it cannot depend on
# which tmux this machine has, which is where the bug hid.
-   '"'*'"') _uq=${_uq#\"}; _uq=${_uq%\"} ;;
+   '"'*'"') return 0 ;;
m did not round-trip

= scan-init-writes-without-a-terminal
f libexec/mux-scan
t mux-scan-init
# A provisioner runs `setup.sh install` on every sweep with no tty. Without
# this branch `read` takes EOF and the default is written unasked, so with no
# ~/src a machine would index its whole HOME: the same defect as the shipped
# `scan ~/src 3`, reached from the other side.
#
# NOTE THE REFUSAL IS NO LONGER UNCONDITIONAL, which this comment used to
# imply. With ~/src PRESENT the no-terminal path deliberately records it, and
# `scan-init-no-terminal-records-src` below guards that half. The split exists
# because $HOME is invented and an existing ~/src is observed.
-     if [ ! -t 0 ] || [ ! -t 1 ]; then
+     if false; then
m must exit 2

= scan-init-no-terminal-records-src
f libexec/mux-scan
t mux-scan-init
# THE OTHER HALF OF THE NO-TERMINAL SPLIT, and the regression it closes was
# reported from a real box. 0.85 removed the shipped `scan ~/src 3`, correctly,
# because a shipped LOCATION fails on every machine that keeps work elsewhere.
# But the replacement could only run at a terminal, and the only install path
# on a provisioned fleet has none: it printed a note nobody read and did
# nothing, so discovery went from working to OFF and `mux go <newrepo>` stopped
# finding anything. Making the refusal unconditional again restores exactly
# that silence.
-       if [ "$_def" = "$HOME" ]; then
+       if true; then
m with ~/src present and no terminal it must record

= scan-init-is-not-idempotent
f libexec/mux-scan
t mux-scan-init
# Running it twice must not give the partition two roots: every scan would then
# cover the same tree twice and the map would read as a mux that cannot count.
-   if [ -n "$_have" ]; then
+   if false; then
m added another root

= scan-init-overwrites-a-config
f libexec/mux-scan
t mux-scan-init
# THE ONE UNRECOVERABLE MISTAKE AVAILABLE HERE. A partition file may already
# carry a label, a theme or a layout, so the bootstrap appends and never
# rewrites. Inverting the test makes it clobber somebody's config.
-   if [ ! -e "$_pf" ]; then
+   if true; then
m DESTROYED an existing setting

= scan-init-hardcodes-src
f libexec/mux-scan
t mux-scan-init
# `~/src` is OFFERED when it exists and never assumed. Hardcoding it is the
# original defect: a location nobody chose, which then failed mux's own check on
# every machine that keeps work somewhere else.
#
# THE KILL MOVED when the no-terminal branch was split by whether ~/src
# exists, and the record was updated rather than the code: hardcoding the
# default now means `$_def` is never equal to `$HOME`, so the refusal is
# skipped entirely and the run dies on `[ -d "$_want" ]` instead. It still
# exits 2, so the exit-code assertion passes and the one about the refusal's
# WORDING is what fires. Reported by the corpus as a wrong-reason kill, which
# the anchor rider cannot see.
-   [ -d "$HOME/src" ] && _def=$HOME/src
+   _def=$HOME/src
m the refusal did not say why

= install-never-mentions-discovery
f setup.sh
t setup
# The installer says when discovery has no roots, and is SILENT once it has
# some. Returning early always makes a new user get a mux whose `mux go <name>`
# finds nothing, with no hint why: the state this step exists to end.
-   "$_bin/$PKG" scan --roots >/dev/null 2>&1 && return 0
+   return 0
m said nothing about discovery

= check-probe-creates-a-server
f libexec/mux-check
t mux-check
# `tmux list-keys` STARTS a server when none is running and answers from tmux's
# DEFAULTS, so probing with it meant the no-server branch could never fire: on a
# bare box `mux check` SPAWNED a server and then reported five FAILs about the
# pristine thing it had just made. Reverting to that probe makes a serverless
# box take the server branch, which the real-tmux case at the end of the test
# catches by asking whether a server now exists.
- if ! _vt list-sessions >/dev/null 2>&1; then
+ if [ -z "$(_vt list-keys -T prefix 2>/dev/null || true)" ]; then
m the check did not say so

= env-plan-truncates-on-an-invalid-name
f libexec/mux-env_lib
t mux-wire
# UNDER `set -e` A BARE NON-ZERO CALL KILLS THE LOOP, and this loop runs inside
# `$( )`, so the plan TRUNCATED at the first INVALID name and every name after
# it alphabetically was never considered. Live on both boxes the day the marker
# shipped: four names resolved, two reported. The fixture's dead name sorts
# FIRST, which is the whole fixture: with the order reversed a truncating loop
# still answers correctly about everything it reached.
-     mux_env_validate "$_epn" "$_epv" || _epvr=$?
+     mux_env_validate "$_epn" "$_epv"
m verdicts for two names

= env-apply-truncates-on-an-invalid-name
f libexec/mux-env_lib
t mux-wire
# THE SAME SHAPE IN THE WORSE PLACE: `bin/mux` calls mux_env_apply on every
# session build and every attach, both under `set -eu`, so ONE dead value
# stopped the repair for every name alphabetically after it and the session
# came up missing pointers mux had resolved perfectly well. Separately
# killable from the plan record because the plan is a different function, so
# the earlier assertion passes and this one fires.
-       mux_env_validate "$_eaname" "$_eahave" || _eavr=$?
+       mux_env_validate "$_eaname" "$_eahave"
m was not applied, so

= update-env-stale-names-a-withheld-value
f libexec/mux-update-env
t mux-update-env
# A PANE IS STALE RELATIVE TO WHAT THE SESSION HOLDS, never to the name list.
# Without this guard every pane was reported stale for a name mux had
# DELIBERATELY WITHHELD: measured live, 18 panes named for WAYLAND_DISPLAY on
# a box where nothing listens to it, advising a restart that could never
# supply it, printed beside a real finding about SSH_AUTH_SOCK. Advice that
# cannot come true is this package's oldest defect class.
-       *) continue ;;
+       *) ;;
m DELIBERATELY WITHHELD

= update-env-dry-run-writes
f libexec/mux-update-env
t mux-update-env
# `-n` MUST GO THROUGH THE SAME DECISION AND ACT ON NOTHING. The flag lives
# inside mux_env_apply so the preview cannot be computed by a different code
# path from the action; losing the -n hand-off makes a dry run WRITE, which is
# worse than having no preview at all, because the caller trusted it. The
# assertion is on the SESSION rather than the output, since "would set" is
# equally printable by a run that also set it.
-   [ "$_dry" -eq 0 ] || _mode=-n
+   _mode=$_mode
m DRY RUN WROTE TO THE SESSION

= update-env-window-refusal-succeeds
f libexec/mux-update-env
t mux-update-env
# A REFUSAL THAT EXITS 0 IS THE SIGNATURE FAILURE OF THIS CODEBASE, and this
# verb shipped with it: the validation used to sit in `_targets`, which the
# pane loop reads through a here-doc, so `exit 2` left only the SUBSHELL. The
# message printed, the substitution came back empty, and the script ran on to
# report "nothing needed changing" and exit 0. Same shape as `mux latch -V`.
# The test asserts the CODE, which is the only observable that differs.
-     exit 2; }
+     : ; }
m a WINDOW field must be refused

= envhook-wayland-probes-its-own-environment
f share/envhooks.d/WAYLAND_DISPLAY
t mux-envhooks
# THE VALUE UNDER TEST MUST BE HANDED TO THE TOOL. A bare `wayland-info`
# probes `$WAYLAND_DISPLAY` from the HOOK's own environment, which is the very
# thing being judged and is routinely absent (a login shell over ssh, a
# systemd unit, an agent pane). It then defaults to wayland-0, finds nothing,
# and calls the display it was ASKED about dead. Measured on both boxes, where
# the live display is wayland-1, and `mux update-env --all` would then have
# removed a working display from every session. The assertion is on what the
# stub was ASKED, not on the verdict: an outcome check passes on the broken
# code wherever the environment happens to name the right display.
-   WAYLAND_DISPLAY=$1 XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=$_d $_wi >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 1
+   $_wi >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 1
m PROBED THE WRONG DISPLAY

= envhook-wayland-guesses-with-no-tool
f share/envhooks.d/WAYLAND_DISPLAY
t mux-envhooks
# NO TOOL IS NOT A VERDICT. The socket exists and nothing on the box can say
# whether anyone is behind it, so the answer is 2 and mux leaves the value
# alone. Answering 1 makes a machine with neither wayland-info nor nc lose a
# working display, which is latch's own recorded trap (a probe site that read
# "cannot tell" as a decision) met from the other side.
- exit 2
+ exit 1
m CANNOT TELL (2)

= update-env-preview-uses-the-past-tense
f libexec/mux-update-env
t mux-update-env
# A PREVIEW MUST READ AS A PREVIEW. mux_env_apply reports what it DID, so
# prefixing "would " onto its words printed `would dropped WAYLAND_DISPLAY`,
# which is how it read on a live box. `set` is the only one of the three that
# works in both tenses, which is exactly why this looked right for a release:
# the common case is the one word that hides it.
-       dropped)  _l="drop ${_l#* }" ;;
+       dropped)  : ;;
m would dropped

= check-notify-daemon-presence-only
f libexec/mux-notify_lib
t mux-check
# A BUS THAT ANSWERS "NOBODY OWNS IT" IS A FINDING, not an unknown. Folding
# `false` into the cannot-tell answer restores the exact bug this probe was
# written for: notify-send present, no daemon owning the name, every
# notification dying with `Message recipient disconnected from message bus
# without replying`, and `mux check` reporting [OK] about it. A presence check
# that passes on a fully installed and fully broken box is the failure the
# marker contract exists to prevent, so having it inside the file that defines
# that contract was the worst available place for it.
-     *false*) return 1 ;;
+     *false*) return 2 ;;
m NOBODY owns the notification

= check-notify-daemon-unasked-is-broken
f libexec/mux-check
t mux-check
# BEING UNABLE TO ASK IS NOT EVIDENCE OF A BROKEN DESKTOP. `mux check` over
# ssh reaches no session bus, so treating the third answer as "nothing will
# display it" is a confident false finding about a machine that is fine: the
# same mistake the WAYLAND_DISPLAY validator shipped the same week, which
# judged its own environment rather than the value it was handed. The test
# asserts the ABSENCE of the warning, because the bug is an extra one.
#
# TARGETED AT THE HEDGE BRANCH rather than at the probe call, because the
# corpus refused the first attempt: setting the verdict directly makes an
# EARLIER case (notify-send with no closer, where the answer is also
# cannot-tell) take the new branch, so the kill landed on the missing-closer
# assertion and proved nothing about this one.
-   elif [ "$_nd" -eq 2 ]; then
+   elif false; then
m no bus to confirm

= send-crosses-a-partition-boundary
f libexec/mux-agent
t mux-agent
# THE SECURITY GATE ITSELF. Every token on a policy line is ANDed, so
# `send-blocked control:agent`, the grant that makes a village of generated
# worker names workable, carries no partition token and reaches agents in
# EVERY partition. A partition is a tmux `-L` socket owned by the login user,
# so same-uid is full reach, and where the boundary is a GROUP acquired per
# session the pane on the far side HOLDS that group: typing into it runs
# commands with privileges this side was refused.
#
# AND THE IDLE FIXTURE IS WHAT MAKES THIS PROVE THE PLACEMENT AS WELL. My
# first version of the gate sat inside `_refuse`, which `idle|working` never
# reach, so it guarded the two rare states and left the common one open.
# `wsess` is idle, so this record SURVIVES against that version: no separate
# record is needed, and one is not expressible as a single-line replacement
# without simply being this one.
-   [ -z "$_scross" ] || _refuse cross-partition ''
+   :
m cross-rc

= send-trusts-a-failed-context-command
f libexec/mux-context_lib
t mux-agent
# FAILING OPEN IS THE ONE ANSWER AN ACTUATING VERB MUST NOT GIVE.
# `mux_ctx_resolve` answers 0 with `global` for a hook that EXITED NON-ZERO
# (measured), so its status alone cannot tell "no hook" from "the hook
# broke", and treating the second as the baseline would let a box whose
# boundary mechanism is broken type anywhere. `MUX_CTX_SRC` is the
# discriminator, and this is the call `mux resume` already makes.
-   if [ "${MUX_CTX_SRC:-baseline}" = failed ]; then
+   if false; then
m ctxfail-rc

= send-compares-the-partition-backwards
f libexec/mux-context_lib
t mux-agent
# BOTH DIRECTIONS IN ONE LINE, which is why the test asserts both: inverting
# it lets a cross-partition send through AND refuses a send inside your own
# partition, so a test that only checked one would read as coverage while the
# feature was deleted in the other direction.
-   [ "$_cxto" = "$_cxown" ] && return 0
+   [ "$_cxto" != "$_cxown" ] && return 0
m send-rc

= addr-leading-sep-accepted
f libexec/mux-addr_lib
t mux-addr
# A `::` WITH NOTHING BEFORE IT NAMES NO PARTITION. Accepting it makes
# `::api` a second spelling of `api`, and one spelling per meaning is the
# entire reason no placeholder is needed: two ways to say one thing is what
# the doubled-separator design bought its way out of.
-     [ -n "$MUX_ADDR_PART" ] || {
+     [ -n x ] || {
m [::api] must be refused

= addr-bare-word-is-not-a-session
f libexec/mux-addr_lib
t mux-addr
# A BARE WORD IS A SESSION, which is the grammar's whole ergonomic claim:
# the common case costs nothing and the RARE field carries the delimiter.
# Dropping the no-dot arm leaves the session empty for every bare name, so
# every caller addressing `api` would act on a whole partition instead.
-   *) _aprest=$_apa ;;
+   *) _aprest= ;;
m session: got

= resume-two-fields-read-as-an-address
f bin/mux
t mux-resume
# THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE ARGUMENT COUNT, NOT EMPTINESS. `mux resume PART ''`
# is a deliberate two-field call naming a partition and no focus, which is
# the shape an older `mux latch` composes; reading it as an address turns it
# into the SESSION `PART`, silently, and that is the whole class of bug this
# grammar change exists to avoid rather than create.
- if [ "$cmd" = resume ] && [ "$#" -le 1 ] && [ -n "$rs_want" ]; then
+ if [ "$cmd" = resume ] && [ -z "$rs_focus" ] && [ -n "$rs_want" ]; then
m EMPTY focus must still resume

= resume-window-field-ignored
f bin/mux
t mux-resume
# A WINDOW IS REFUSED RATHER THAN IGNORED. resume rebuilds a session SET and
# lands on one of them, so there is nothing per-window for it to do, and a
# field it silently drops is a flag that parses and does nothing: worse than
# a missing one, because the caller believes it asked for something.
-   [ -z "$MUX_ADDR_WIN" ] || {
+   [ -n x ] || {
m window field must be refused

= resume-address-drops-the-session
f bin/mux
t mux-resume
# The session half of the one-argument address. Losing it makes every
# addressed resume rebuild the set and land nowhere, which looks like success
# and is the `mux latch box` bug this package already shipped once: a default
# that succeeded at the wrong thing and said nothing.
-   rs_focus=$MUX_ADDR_SESS
+   rs_focus=
m nothing was switched to it

= sess-name-colon-silently-rewritten
f bin/mux
t mux-rename
# A NAME THE USER TYPED IS REFUSED, never quietly rewritten. mux RESERVES the
# colon for addressing, and handing back a different session from the one
# asked for is this package's signature failure: plausible, wrong and silent.
# The fold is still there for a DERIVED name, so deleting the refusal does not
# break anything loudly, it just gives you `a-b` when you asked for `a:b`.
-   mux_sess_name_clean "$_new" || {
+   [ -n x ] || {
m must be refused with 2

= sess-name-rule-refuses-an-address
f bin/mux
t mux-resume
# THE RESERVATION MUST NOT EAT THE GRAMMAR IT PROTECTS. This block is the
# SHARED name resolution, so without the `resume` exclusion the colon rule
# refused `work::api`, which is the qualified ADDRESS form and the whole
# reason the colon is reserved in the first place. Caught by the suite within
# a minute of being written.
-   [ "$cmd" = resume ] || mux_sess_name_clean "$name_arg" || {
+   mux_sess_name_clean "$name_arg" || {
m an unknown partition should exit 3

= sess-name-clean-accepts-a-colon
f libexec/mux-sessions_lib
t mux-rename
# THE RULE ITSELF, in the one place it now lives. It was open-coded as
# `tr ':.' '--'` in FIVE places in bin/mux with no test anywhere, which is
# the shape that drifts; a mutation here reaches every caller at once.
-   *:*) return 1 ;;
+   *:*) return 0 ;;
m must be refused with 2

= check-blind-to-an-unaddressable-session
f libexec/mux-check
t mux-check
# THE GAP THE REFUSALS LEAVE: a session made with RAW tmux, which only became
# possible at all in tmux 3.7 (it sanitises session names NOT AT ALL, where
# 3.4 and 3.6 fold a colon). Nothing else in the audit can see it, and the
# session works perfectly while being impossible to name in an address, so
# this is the marker contract's "fully installed and fully broken" shape
# narrowed to one name.
-     mux_sess_name_clean "$_s" || _unaddr="$_unaddr[$_s] "
+     :
m cannot be named in

= log-header-not-gated-on-a-terminal
f libexec/mux-log
t mux-log
# THE QUIET HALF IS THE CONTRACT. The path is not log content, so a pipe, a
# redirect and a `2>&1` capture must all be byte-identical to before.
# Ungating this was the FIRST version and the suite killed it immediately: an
# earlier case in the same file counts `-n 2` through a helper that folds
# stderr into stdout, and it saw three lines. Any real consumer reading
# `mux log 2>&1` would gain a line the same way.
#
# THE KILL LANDS ON THAT PRE-EXISTING CASE rather than on the one written for
# this feature, and the `m` names it deliberately: an EXISTING consumer
# breaking is better evidence than a new assertion agreeing with its author.
- [ -t 2 ] && printf 'mux: log at %s\n' "$_f" >&2
+ printf 'mux: log at %s\n' "$_f" >&2
m did not return 2 lines

= readme-clobbers-a-human-edit
f setup.sh
t setup
# THE ONE UNRECOVERABLE MISTAKE AVAILABLE HERE. The README is mux-generated
# and refreshed on install, which is only safe while the marker line is the
# gate: strip the marker and the file is the user's. Without this guard an
# install silently overwrites whatever somebody wrote in its place, which is
# the same failure `mux setup claude` is careful about with another program's
# config, and worse than a stale README by a wide margin.
#
# RE-ANCHORED when test/lint.t refused the line for a missing `--` on a grep
# pattern taken from a variable: the dash-name rule, catching its own author.
-      && ! grep -qxF -- "$_README_MARK" "$_cr_f" 2>/dev/null; then
+      && false; then
m OVERWROTE a README

= paths-config-ignores-the-override
f setup.sh
t setup
# THE VERB MUST DESCRIBE THE INSTALL IT WAS ASKED ABOUT, not the developer's
# own machine. `paths` is the single declaration four other things consume
# (the install audit, the stale-path sweep, uninstall saying what it kept,
# and discoverability), so a root it reports wrongly is four consumers acting
# on the wrong path. Hardcoding the default is the easy way to get that wrong
# and it passes any test run with no overrides set.
#
# THE KILL LANDS ON `uninstall`, EARLIER IN THE FILE THAN THE `paths` CASE,
# and that is the guard working rather than a weak record: uninstall names the
# config root it kept from this same declaration, so a hardcoded root is
# wrong THERE first. Four consumers, one declaration, which is the whole
# argument for the verb; the corpus found the coupling by killing through it.
-   printf 'config\t%s\n'  "${MUX_DIR:-${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/$PKG}"
+   printf 'config\t%s\n'  "$HOME/.config/$PKG"
m did not NAME the config root it left behind

= paths-drops-a-root
f setup.sh
t setup
# A ROOT THE CONTRACT DOES NOT NAME IS A ROOT NOTHING SWEEPS. The stale-path
# audit and uninstall both walk this list, so a dropped line is a directory
# that survives every migration and every uninstall silently, which is the
# `~/.config/mux.bak` crumb mechanised.
-   printf 'payload\t%s\n' "$_shr/$PKG"
+   :
m never reports the 'payload' root

= setup-reload-knows-the-new-tilde-form
f setup.sh
t setup
# THE SECOND TILDE SPELLING, and it needs its own record because four forms
# are accepted (expanded or tilde, old path or new) and a fixture using one
# leaves the rest unkillable: the corpus said so by surviving a mutation of
# this very line while the old-spelling fixture carried the case. The test
# drives both tilde forms in a loop now.
-   _rt="~${_shr#"$HOME"}/$PKG/share/mux.tmux"
+   _rt=/nowhere/at/all
m TILDE [share/mux/share/mux.tmux]

= venv-default-outside-the-payload
f indicator/setup.sh
t mux-indicator-setup
# THE VENV FOLDS INTO MUX'S PAYLOAD (fleet install-placement rule, ruled
# 2026-10-01). `~/.venvs` had the same ours-only smell as the
# `~/.local/libexec` that ruling struck: a root at the top of $HOME with ten
# tenants, none of them anybody else's. Restoring the old default leaves the
# package spread over two roots, which is the thing one-payload-tree exists
# to end, and it passes any test that pins MUX_INDICATOR_VENV.
- VENV=${MUX_INDICATOR_VENV:-$_mux_pay/venv}
+ VENV=${MUX_INDICATOR_VENV:-$HOME/.venvs/mux-indicator}
m not inside mux's payload

= venv-leftover-unreported
f indicator/setup.sh
t mux-indicator-setup
# `install` REMOVES THE OLD VENV ONLY AFTER A SUCCESSFUL REBUILD, because a
# failed rebuild that had already deleted it would leave the box with no
# indicator at all and the running daemon as the last copy. So a box that has
# not re-run install yet legitimately has TWO, and the only thing that makes
# the stale one visible is this line. A WARN, never a FAIL: nothing resolves
# through it once the bin link moves, so it is wasted disk rather than breakage.
-     && warn "retired venv survives: $OLD_VENV (install removes it)" || :
+     && : || :
m was not reported

= runtime-path-not-namespaced
f libexec/mux-agent-state_lib
t mux-agent-summary
# THE NAMESPACE (fleet install-placement rule, 2026-10-01). Per-pane state
# used to sit at `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/agent-state`, beside `at-spi`, `bus`,
# `dbus-1`, `dconf`, `doc` and `gcr`, under a GENERIC name any other agent
# tool could claim. Reverting it also resurrects the retired path on a box
# that never had one, which is what the fresh-box assertion catches.
#
# THE KILL LANDS ON A PRE-EXISTING CASE, named deliberately: with the
# namespace gone every fixture in this file writes where nothing reads, so
# the headless summary answers `none 0`. An existing consumer breaking is
# better evidence than a new assertion agreeing with its author.
-   _ad=$_rt/mux/agent-state/$_ns
+   _ad=$_rt/agent-state/$_ns
m headless: got [none 0]

= install-adopts-live-records
f setup.sh
t setup
# ADOPTION IS THE HALF THAT MATTERS, and it is invisible on a fresh box: with
# it gone, a box upgrading mid-session keeps its records at the old path
# while every reader looks at the new one, so EVERY LIVE PANE draws the
# no-record glyph until its agent's next lifecycle hook fires. On an idle
# session that is hours. A regression caused by a tidy-up is the worst kind,
# and no assertion about a clean install can see it.
#
# IT MOVED HERE FROM `mux_agent_dir` (2026-10-01) because a READER that
# migrates takes a snapshot: see that lib's comment for the measured cost.
# The replacement keeps the counter so the step still reports, which is what
# makes the fixture's own assertion the thing that fires.
-         mv -- "$_r" "$_nd/" 2>/dev/null && _moved=$((_moved + 1))
+         _moved=$((_moved + 1))
m was not adopted

= install-adoption-overwrites-a-live-record
f setup.sh
t setup
# THE OTHER DIRECTION, and the dangerous one. A destination that already
# exists is the box that has ALREADY taken a snapshot, or one whose new
# writer has already run; either way the installer cannot tell which, because
# a beat refreshes mtime without advancing the epoch, so the stale copy can
# legitimately be the newer FILE. Overwriting therefore destroys a live
# record about as often as it repairs a dead one. Two guards, two records:
# this one is `rm the source`, the one above is `move it`.
-         rm -f -- "$_r" && _kept=$((_kept + 1))
+         mv -- "$_r" "$_nd/" && _kept=$((_kept + 1))
