= install-never-calls-the-adoption
f setup.sh
t setup
# THE CALL SITE, which neither record above can see: a function defined and
# never called passes every assertion about its own body. It shares the
# failure message with `install-adopts-live-records` on purpose, because the
# OBSERVABLE is the same (nothing was adopted) and only the cause differs.
-   _adopt_runtime_state
+   :
m was not adopted

= agent-dir-adopts-from-a-reader
f libexec/mux-agent-state_lib
t mux-agent-rank
# THE BUG THAT SHIPPED, 2026-10-01, reintroduced in one line because that is
# all it ever was. Eleven of this function's twelve callers are read-only, so
# the first of them to run on an upgraded box copied the old state tree, the
# still-deployed old writer carried on updating the original, and at the next
# install the frozen copy became authoritative: a session reading `working`
# with an epoch 51 minutes stale, every beat refreshing it, no transition
# logged because there was nothing left to transition from.
#
# THE ASSERTION IS AN ABSENCE, which is the only thing that can see this: the
# returned PATH was always correct, and a timestamp cannot tell the snapshot
# from the live record afterwards. Adoption belongs to `setup.sh`, where the
# install is atomic with the writer switching over.
-   printf '%s' "$_ad"
+   [ -d "$_rt/agent-state/$_ns" ] && mkdir -p "$_ad"; printf '%s' "$_ad"
m adopted the old state path

= install-event-not-logged
f setup.sh
t setup
# THE ANCHOR EVENT. A provisioner swallows setup.sh's output entirely
# (measured 2026-10-01: its log carried `install: pkg mux -> ~/.local` and not
# one of mux's own notices), so without this line nothing on the box can say
# which mux it received or when. These notes have twice had to reconstruct
# that from a payload mtime against a provisioner log, which only works while
# both survive.
-   _slog "installed $("$_bin/$PKG" -V 2>/dev/null || echo "$PKG ?") to $_pay"
+   :
m the install EVENT is not logged

= adoption-repair-advice-not-logged
f setup.sh
t setup
# THE ONE NOTICE NO LATER CHECK CAN RECONSTRUCT, which is why it is logged
# while the fragment and scan-root notices are not: the adoption happens
# ONCE, and a record kept at the new path is unjudged forever afterwards. No
# timestamp rule can tell a stale snapshot from a live record (a beat
# refreshes mtime without advancing the epoch), so the repair verb is the
# whole answer and it has to reach somebody.
-     _slog "kept $_kept record(s) unjudged; mux agent-doctor --repair"; }
+     }
m kept a record unjudged and said so only on stdout

= control-class-not-written
f bin/mux
t mux-pane-class
# THE PRODUCER ITSELF. `libexec/mux-agent` reads `@mux-control` in two places
# to decide whether a send may proceed, and before this nothing in the tree
# wrote it, so the send policy's `control:agent` grant was unreachable: a
# root-owned line nobody could satisfy. With this gone it is unreachable
# again, and silently, because a pane with no option reads as `human`, which
# is a REFUSAL and therefore looks like the policy working.
-     tm set-option -p -t "$thispane" "@mux-$verb" "$rest"
+     :
m there was no @mux-control call at all

= control-class-marks-the-wrong-pane
f bin/mux
t mux-pane-class
# MARKING THE WRONG PANE IS THE SIGNATURE FAILURE: plausible, wrong, silent.
# `control` qualifies the pane just declared, so the builder must use the
# pane it last created; `$firstpane` is a real variable in scope and the
# mutation is one word. A fixture answering `%1` to every pane_id query
# could not tell these apart, which is why this file's stub counts panes
# rather than queries.
-     tm set-option -p -t "$thispane" "@mux-$verb" "$rest"
+     tm set-option -p -t "$firstpane" "@mux-$verb" "$rest"
