= control-class-accepts-any-word
f libexec/mux-class_lib
t mux-pane-class
# AN UNKNOWN WORD MUST BE LOUD. Accepting it writes an option every reader
# falls through on, so the pane reads as `human`: the layout would read as a
# grant and behave as a refusal, which is the worst available answer. Same
# call `notify` makes about its two words and `mux agent-hook` makes about an
# unknown event: an UNSET thing says nothing because nobody asked, a NAMED
# one mux cannot honour is an author claiming a contract that does not exist.
-   human|agent|hybrid) return 0 ;;
+   human|agent|hybrid|supervisor) return 0 ;;
m was accepted and should be refused

= control-class-lost-on-save
f bin/mux
t mux-save
# THE ROUND TRIP, and the same defect `@mux-agent` two fields earlier already
# exists to prevent. Dropping this emits a layout with no class, so the next
# build hands the pane to the human: a supervisor can no longer type into its
# own worker and nothing anywhere says why. A save that quietly writes LESS
# than it read is this file's whole subject.
-     agent|hybrid) printf 'control %s\n' "$_pk" ;;
+     agent|hybrid) ;;
m came back unclassed

= pane-class-refusal-names-the-wrong-directive
f bin/mux
t mux-pane-class
# THE ONE RISK OF A SHARED PARSER ARM. `control` and `attention` are two
# markers answering two questions, read by different consumers, and they
# share one validator arm only because they happen to take the same three
# words. The arm reports `$verb` so a mistake names the line the author
# actually wrote; hardcoding either directive sends the reader to the wrong
# one, which is the whole cost of merging them and the reason this is
# guarded rather than trusted.
-       echo "mux: '$verb' with no pane in $profile" >&2
+       echo "mux: 'control' with no pane in $profile" >&2
