= attention-class-lost-on-save
f bin/mux
t mux-save
# THE SECOND MARKER'S ROUND TRIP, and its own record rather than a shared
# one, because the two are emitted by two `case` arms on purpose: a pane can
# declare one and not the other, and the combination that proves it (a
# worker its supervisor may type into AND the human should still see) is
# exactly what a folded emitter would make unsayable. Dropping this hands a
# worker back to the human's strip, notifications and next-blocked on the
# next build: the inversion R2 exists to prevent.
-     agent|hybrid) printf 'attention %s\n' "$_pn" ;;
+     agent|hybrid) ;;
m 'attention agent' was dropped

= strip-shows-a-worker-to-the-human
f libexec/mux-agent-state-render
t mux-strip
# vicus's R2, on the surface it names first. A worker blocked on its
# SUPERVISOR would otherwise paint its whole session blocked, so one
# checkpoint makes a project look stuck and the human goes looking for a
# decision nobody wanted from them. The skip set is built from a marker that
# rides a query the strip was already making, so the filter costs nothing and
# removing it costs the whole promise.
-   _pair=$(mux_agent_state_skip "$dir" "$sess" "$_notours")
+   _pair=$(mux_agent_state "$dir" "$sess")
m painted the human's chip for bravo

= strip-filters-the-record-but-not-the-plug
f libexec/mux-agent-state-render
t mux-strip
# THE SECOND PATH, and the reason the test asserts twice. A pane is excluded
# from TWO questions: whether a RECORD speaks for the session, and whether
# mux STARTED an agent there. Filter only the records and a worker-only
# session draws the UNWIRED plug forever: the loudest glyph on the strip,
# announcing the one agent the human was told to ignore. Neither assertion
# kills this alone, which is the two-guards pattern again.
-   | awk -F'\t' '$2 != "" && $3 != "agent" { print $4 }')
+   | awk -F'\t' '$2 != "" { print $4 }')
m drawing the UNWIRED plug

= next-blocked-offers-a-worker-to-the-human
f libexec/mux-next-blocked
t mux-next-blocked
# THE PROMISE IS "take me to whoever needs me most", so handing the human an
# orchestrated worker that is waiting on its supervisor is not a near miss, it
# is the promise inverted. The fixture carries a control (the same records
# with no attention declared DO offer the older wait), so the kill is the
# marker's doing rather than a fixture that cannot fail.
-   pair=$(mux_agent_state_skip "$dir" "$s" "$_notours")
+   pair=$(mux_agent_state "$dir" "$s")
m was offered to the human

= notification-rings-for-a-worker
f libexec/mux-agent-state-emit
t mux-notify
# A BANNER IS THE ONE SURFACE THAT INTERRUPTS, which is why R2 calls this the
# worse half: a run with several workers rings the bell repeatedly for
# decisions the human was never going to make. The supervisor is the thing
# that is waiting and it already knows. Three cases guard it together (agent
# silent, undeclared notifies, hybrid notifies), so a mutation that merely
# silenced everything dies on the control rather than passing.
-     _always=0; _vis=1
+     _always=0
