= window-bare-word-is-a-session
f libexec/mux-window
t mux-window
# THE REINTERPRETATION, and the one place mux's address grammar is read from
# the other end. The shared parser reads a bare word as a SESSION, because
# every other verb's subject is one; this verb's subject is a WINDOW, so the
# last field is the name. Without the swap `mux window build` asks for a
# session called `build` and opens a window there: it SUCCEEDS, at the wrong
# thing, which is the 0.32 `mux latch HOST` bug exactly.
#
# THE MUTATION KEEPS THE SESSION rather than dropping the swap, because the
# arity guard below (`[ -n "$_wname" ] || _usage`) catches a missing name and
# turns every simpler mutation into a usage error, which proves nothing about
# WHICH field the word became.
-   _wsess=
+   _wsess=$_wsess
